Revision history of WwW-SiteConf


0.03_00  2026-11-12  PULLED
    BIG Unicode (I18N) update!

    *** File names, arguments and messages are spelled as
        RdKw::I18N::CodeSets says, which is required now (and
        Encode::Locale no longer is, but by it): as the locale
        says, but for what SITECONF_CODESETS says -- in siteconf,
        configure and render alike.  configure and render take
        -C codeset too, after it, for the whole command line.
        configure checks for CodeSets, not Encode::Locale.
    * render reads its options with Getopt::Long (-B, -C; still
      -- before the files), not Getopt::Std.
    + siteconf -V -V: I18N::CodeSets' version, too.
    + siteconf -l (list) [-F manifest] [-t [goal][:NEED|:WANT]]
      [-0[octal]]: the names a goal of the manifest NEEDs
      and WANTs (the default one, both, if not said), once
      each, unescaped, and as the file system spells them
      (a name it cannot is an error, not \x{...}), each
      ended by a newline, or what -0 says (a NUL; -072, :),
      for tar --null -T or xargs -0.
    + MANIFEST: a NEED or WANT is unescaped as a header is
      (${$}, ${U+hex}; read by GenGenLib's read_manifest);
      what that comes to is checked as it was.  A $ in one
      that is no escape is an error of its own now.
    + A ; in a rule ends its prerequisites, and what follows
      is the first line of its recipe (a: b ; cp b a), as it
      is to make; a tab line after it is the next.  It used
      to be read as more prerequisites.
    + -P tem: a Template Toolkit render with D and O, and no
      web (no GenLib, no site_url, no location).  It needs
      GenGenLib 0.0100 (and so may not be pinned below it).
    + $$ or ${$} in a header is a $, and ${U+hex} the character
      of that code point (${U+0020}, a space), as they are to
      GenGenLib.  So a header can name any file, and so may
      render be named (its HAVE is escaped; with what is not
      ASCII left as it is, but for white space).
    *** :Q in a recipe: a $ in what it makes is the shell's,
        and is written $$, so that make (or ninja) passes it on
        rather than expanding it (as bmake's :q).  A $ from $$
        or a variable is make's, as ever.
    + -G gmake: a Makefile for GNU make (newer than 3.81),
      which can name more: a $ is $$; blanks, #, : and | are
      escaped with a backslash; wildcards are in brackets
      (a[*]b), and a ~ in front is ./~.  A line break, ; or =,
      a \ at the end, or (...) at the end is still an error.
    *** -G make: a name with white space, a control, a $, any
        of #:;=|(){}[]*?, a \ at the end or a ~ in front is an
        error (saying whether -G gmake could), rather than a
        Makefile that is wrong: there is no escaping in POSIX
        make, and bmake reads $$ twice.  -G ninja: a line break
        or a | is an error (ninja cannot escape either).
    + -MRdKw::WwW::GenGenLib::Template::Provider (of its own
      dist, WwW-GenGenLib-Template-Provider) has render load
      templates through it: only what the page declares, and
      what that declares, and so on.  Only if asked for: no
      preset asks, and render is the same as ever without it.
    *** Perl 5.8 is required, and so is Encode::Locale (by
        siteconf, the configure it writes, and GenGenLib 0.0100,
        which is required too).
    *** File names are characters, spelled as the file system's
        locale says (locale_fs) only on their way to it, and an
        error if it cannot spell them: Makefile.in, MANIFEST,
        headers, -B, -o, and what configure makes and finds.
        What is in the files is UTF-8, as it always was -- the
        Makefile and build.ninja too, so a name that is not ASCII
        is found by make or ninja only where locale_fs is UTF-8.
    *** Arguments are decoded as the locale says (decode_argv),
        and an error if they cannot be; they used to be UTF-8
        or else left alone.
    * Messages are written as the locale says, with \x{...} for
      what it cannot say, by siteconf, configure and render
      (configure wrote UTF-8, and siteconf whatever perl had).
    * configure always checks for Encode::Locale, and for
      GenGenLib 0.0100 unless told otherwise (-M).  render
      decodes its arguments and names its files for Template
      as the locale does; pin -MRdKw::WwW::GenGenLib=0.0002 if
      you do not want this.
    *** Makefile.in and MANIFEST are split into words at white
        space as make knows it (space, tab, newline, VT, FF,
        CR) and at nothing else: a no-break space is part of a
        name now, as it is to read_deps (GenGenLib 0.0100), so
        render may be named with one.  So are :T, :S and the
        like, and ninja's recipes.
    + Prerequisites in Makefile.in may say NEED:x (the same as
      x) or WANT:x (kept if it can be had, dropped with a
      warning if not, like a WANT in a header).  In capitals,
      with no space after the colon; any other prefix is an
      error (NEED:NEED: for a file named `NEED:').
    *** So a prerequisite with a colon before any `/' or `$'
        (other than those two prefixes) is an error now.
    * "A (which is B)" is uniformly replaced by "A (aka B)",
      which despite being a three-letter abbreviation for
      "also known as", is shorter, and possibly helps non-
      English speakers read better than relative clauses. :)
      (Directly inspired by C compiler diagnostic messages
      (like clang's).  Of course, there is also plan for
      gettext translation like Debian adduser... but this
      will do the trick for now.)
    * reworked comments in generated scripts


0.02_03  2026-11-09  RC
    * Have render recommend $< / ${.IMPSRC} (i meant to do
      this yesterday or something but i forgot it lol u_u)
    *** Require GenGenLib 0.0003 (norm_dep (with pass_thru)
        and uproot), which also tell the source of a rule and
        the tree of a target in Makefile.in now: any spelling
        of ${A}/${a}/, ${B}/${b}/ and the like that GenGenLib
        understands is understood here, and any number of
        slashes after (but NOT between) the symbols.

    *** Multiple slashes may not follow ${A}; such a path would
        be treated as an absolute path beginning with "/${a}"
        and loses its symbolic meaning as a source file.

    + A first prerequisite of ${a} alone (or ${a}/, ${A}/${a}//,
      ...) suppresses the implicit ${a}/${@} prerequisite without
      naming a source in its place.  The target has none; ${<} in
      its recipe is an error.  (A lone ${a} used to be an ordinary
      prerequisite on the directory.)

    * render now assumes GenLib 0.02_03 for variadic locate;
      pin with -MRdKw::WwW::GenLib=<version earlier than 0.0203>
      if you do not want this.

0.02_02  2026-11-07  RC
    * Better render (with the bug-fixed GenGenLib 0.0002!);
      pin with -MRdKw::WwW::GenGenLib=<version earlier than 0.0002>
      if you do not want this.
    * Hard indirect prerequisites stay hard (thanks Claude)
    * Misc error/warning message tweaks

0.02_01  2026-11-06  RC
    + -Dclean now names the cleaning rule(s) of the Makefile.
      The default is `clean', which generates one merged clean
      rule as before.  With an & in it, say -Dclean='clean-&',
      each goal has a rule of its own (make clean-<goal>) which
      removes only what that goal needs.  A lone -Dclean='&'
      suppresses generation of any clean rules.

      *** The `clean' rule no longer removes .ok (-De).

    + Comment lines may sit inside a continued rule header of
      Makefile.in, between the backslashed lines; they are skipped.

    + More modifiers for Makefile.in!  (Modifiers.pm)
      Borrowed from BSD make(1):

        :E :H :R :T   suffix, dirname, all but suffix, basename
        :S/old/new/   with ^old, old$, &, and the flags 1, g, W;
                      not a regex.  old and new may have ${...}.

      Modifiers chain (${@:R:Q}).  Unlike bmake, a dot in a
      directory is not a suffix: ${a.b/c:R} is a.b/c.

    + ${@} and ${.TARGET} now work in the prerequisites of a rule,
      so targets that share a rule can name their sources:

        a.md.html b.md.html: ${A}/${a}/${@:R}

      This depends each Markdown-derived HTML file in ${b} on
      its Markdown source (a.md and b.md) in ${a} respectively.

    *** Makefile.in: a first prerequisite in ${a} (${A}/${a}/...
        or ${a}/..., as written) now IS the source of the target,
        So writing `styles.css: ${a}/styles.scss' depends on the
        SaSS source, without demanding ${a}/styles.css to exist.

        In cases like these the implicit ${a}/${@} dependency
        must be named explicitly.

    + Added ${<} or ${.IMPSRC} as the source version of ${@} or
      ${.TARGET}.  This path is relative to ${a}.  (Ironically
      this IMPSRC is not so implicit given the above mechanism,
      but I gotta pay homage to where I got the name from. :/)

    *** configure only creates the leaf directory of ${B}
        if missing.  To create intermediates use mkdir -p
        or pass --mkpath (for old behavior).

    + An alternative to ${b} is added: the cache, ${c} (-Dc).
      A rule may make an intermediary fragment there which the
      site comprises, but does not include directly, by naming
      it ${c}/FILE.  Targets are still limited to one of these
      two prefixes.  Targets without a prefix default to ${b},
      retaining backward-compatibility.

      (It is also okay to explicitly specify ${B}/${b} and
      ${B}/${c}, but we won't ask for that here. ;)

      Trying to `siteconf -g' on a project without a `c' is
      an error (though you could just duplicate the `b' line
      and change it to `c'.)

    ! ${b} and ${c} may collide (like before with ${a} and ${b})
      and there are no checks for it.  Please use names in ${c}
      sparingly without treating it as a separate namespace! :(

      In fact, the value of -Dc is "glued" to -Db by default,
      so that you should feel comfortable using them as semantic
      indicators without fear of creating an unwanted parallel
      build tree.  More power to us! :P

    + The header of a source file that a header NEEDs (or WANTs) is
      read as well, and so on, much as gcc -MM lists the headers of
      headers: what NEEDs a template NEEDs what the template does.
      What we make ourselves is not followed (that is for its own
      rule, and the build tool's ordering, to say).

    * Expansion of names in dependency headers is no longer limited
      to A a B b c (so that it's consistent with the (new) subs_deps
      function in GenGenLib 0.00_02. :)

0.02_00  2026-11-02  RC
    + siteconf -i preset improvement!
      For the Web preset you can suppress render template output
      with -o render:/dev/null.  You can specify a different
      name in general, e.g. say you organize utilities in a
      directory, then you can use -o render:gen/render.

      The render template will adapt to the name you choose,
      so it would read `# HAVE: gen/render' in the header.

      !!! Note that this file is (still) generated relative to
          the project root, and (still) needs a sane name since
          it needs to be accounted for in the dep graph.  I *try*
          to be forgiving with this, but in general keep it ASCII,
          URL-safe and free of whitespace (ESPECIALLY \x{20} and
          any C0 controls).

      *** The template name is omissible and defaults to configure
          UNLESS your target file contains a colon, in which case
          you must now write `-o configure:weird:path'.

      *** siteconf -i also no longer clobbers configure by default.
          You must pass -f, or (in a script) pass -q and read the
          exit status to see if configure was ever updated.

    * general configure manual: RdKw::WwW::SiteConf::Configure.
      Written by GPT-6 based on man/configure.pod from cooper-www.

    *** A file named `render' in the project directory no longer
        depends on ${B}/MY/option.mk implicitly.  This was already
        the case in www-cellar 87aa96e7ace3f96d1595ad26b1b71a175f746abe
        (Un-bake MY/option.mk as a dependency of render, on Aug 21)
        so i'm not sure where Claude got that from when it generated
        commit 0f20f3f31c51af9d0725a0e12c6c6c3f4fb12a67 (Oct 30)....
        It is "breaking" nonetheless if you had an older render script
        but I think it is for the better. :v

    + Prerequisites of a rule (the renderer or anything outside
      ${a} in general*) may join the dep graph too!  Their NEEDs
      and WANTs are also parsed.  (This is how the Web preset's
      render still depends on ${B}/MY/option.mk, in spite of the
      removal of special-casing above.)
          * any prerequisite that `siteconf' does not build
            is processed; they don't have to be outside ${a}
            specifically.  Thanks Claude for the correction

        *** If such a file says what it HAVEs, that must agree
            with what the rules call it.  A render you moved or
            renamed by hand still says `HAVE: render'; fix that
            line (or perhaps remove it, but well... your choice.)

    + ${A} is no longer special.  You can now ./configure -DA=...
      to use a different project root (though that project should
      contain all the files you NEED in your MANIFEST, obviously!)

      * An absolute A is kept as is in MY/config.mk, like B is.
        Only a *relative* A remains computed as a path relative
        to the build directory.  This is so that people can use
        absolute paths to certify that it is safe and probably
        shorter for cd -- ${A:Q} too.

      * BUG fix: ${A}/... in a header is now really the same as without
        it, even when ${a} is absolute; and a tool's HAVE agrees with a
        rule's ${A}/... when A is absolute.  (Thanks Claude)

    *** ./configure no longer resolves the project root (A, from
        -SA) relative to itself.  You must change into configure's
        own directory or override -DA; i.e., do `cd /my/proj &&
        ./configure' or `/my/proj/configure -DA=/my/proj'.  (A
        nice way to convince yourself is to see if configure could
        still make sense if you run `perl </path/to/configure';
        it wouldn't (and frankly should not) know where it is. :)

    * update %v capitalization in the render comment consistently
      (regressed in v0.01_03~6 "2c3893f8712d48d7ebf25ea2ce9919b843f73125")


0.01_03  2026-11-01  RC
    Template update. :[

    * don't clobber %O with location
    * more transparent render script

0.01_02  2026-11-01  RC
    * Makefile.in target and dependency lists support backslash
      continuations across multiple lines.
    * BUG fix: -Db=. is now supported (though with degraded
      diagnostic messages, it's better than completely broken)

0.01_01  2026-10-31  RC
    * siteconf -g can now -o-utput to a file different from the
      backend default (such as Makefile.proj instead of Makefile).
      It can also override manifest and ruleset with -F and -R.
    *** the manifest (F) and the rules (R) are now paths from
      the current directory, the same for -F, -R and the configured
      -DF, -DR; they used to be relative to the project root (A).
      They are not part of the source tree.  To keep the old meaning,
      write the path from where you run `siteconf -g' (e.g. ../MANIFEST).
      (But I mean, the old meaning wasn't even intentional; so...)
    * everyone understands -q now!
    * siteconf -g tooltip (similar to what ./configure does)
    * siteconf -i now writes the options it received (similar to
      how BIND 9 dig +cmd prints the options at the top).  should
      be helpful to folks who need to re-generate it over updates.


0.01_00  2026-10-31  RC
    this is mostly a clean-up of AI-generated oddities
    i didn't like, but nonetheless breaking so i bumped
    the first two digits.

    + non-web-specific logic has been extracted to GenGenLib.
      (we do not depend on GenLib.)

    + -V for printing versions.  double -V for library versions
      (possibly useful for bug hunting)

    *** the web preset now generates stock render-er.  it does
      not generate the Makefile.in that makes it useful though.

    + patch-friendly siteconf -i -D and -O help text


0.00_00  2026-10-31  RC
    !>! written with substantial help from Anthropic Claude !<!

    first release!

    * siteconf -i (init) writes ./configure from a template and
      a preset (-P web).  This ./configure script has been based
      on www-cellar (which was written by me, at least the core
      of it), which was a re-design of the cooper-www experiment.

      The text is deterministic, so local changes may be
      kept as a patch on top of it.

    * ./configure writes MY/config.mk (definitions, the D
      namespace) and MY/option.mk (options, the O namespace).

    * siteconf -g (make) reads those and writes the build file,
      -G make (Makefile) or -G ninja (build.ninja), and MY/
      MANIFEST.<goal> for each goal (aka phony target).

    * MANIFEST is MAKE/NEED/WANT.  A WANT is resolved at
      configuration time: dropped, with a warning, unless
      it can be had.

    * Headers (HAVE/NEED/WANT) name paths from the project
      root: ${A}/${a}/index.html, ${B}/${b}/style.css, ${B}/
      MY/option.mk.  Depending on something in ${B}/${b} is
      an edge to that file, which is made first (cycles are
      an error).
